(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-ent-pathophysiology-quiz"]={"title":"ENT Pathophysiology Quiz 1 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Middle ear effusion and cholesteatoma impair conduction by which mechanism?","choices":["Obstruction","Mass loading","Stiffness effect","Discontinuity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both add fluid or tissue weight that damps movement of the tympanic membrane and ossicles. The chain is intact and mobile in principle, but it is carrying extra mass. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which statement describes cochlear damage from long-term chronic noise exposure?","choices":["Above 85 decibels, by stereocilia degeneration and apoptosis","Above 85 decibels, causing reversible stereocilia fatigue","Above 120 decibels, causing tympanic membrane rupture","Above 60 decibels, causing endolymphatic hydrops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Long-term chronic exposure to sound above 85 decibels induces irreversible stereocilia degeneration and apoptosis of the acoustic hair cells. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which description fits conductive hearing loss?","choices":["Destruction of hair cells or auditory nerve fibers, in the inner ear","Failure of glutamate release at cranial nerve VIII","Defective sound transmission, in the external or middle ear","Central processing failure in the auditory cortex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The apparatus that carries sound inward has failed; the cochlea and nerve are intact. That anatomical split is what the tuning fork tests are detecting. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why does asymmetrical vestibular input produce a false sense of motion?","choices":["The cochlea sends conflicting auditory signals","The brain reads the mismatch between the two sides as movement","Cerebral perfusion falls on one side","The otoliths dissolve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Balance is a comparison, so unequal input is indistinguishable from turning. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the pattern of onset in presbycusis?","choices":["Gradual, symmetrical and bilateral","Sudden and unilateral","Episodic and fluctuating","Gradual but strictly unilateral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is sensorineural. Asymmetry would point away from aging toward a focal lesion. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How do the causes of anterior and posterior epistaxis differ?","choices":["Anterior from local trauma and dry air; posterior from hypertension and anticoagulation","Anterior from hypertension and anticoagulation; posterior from digital trauma","Both are caused predominantly by anticoagulation","Anterior from coagulopathy; posterior from low humidity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anterior bleeds are local and mechanical; posterior bleeds reflect a systemic vascular or clotting problem, which is also why they are more profuse and carry airway risk. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which demographic and inheritance profile fits otosclerosis?","choices":["Commonest in older males, with no hereditary component","Commonest in children, autosomal recessive","Young-to-middle-aged females, accelerated by pregnancy; 50% autosomal dominant","Commonest in young females but entirely sporadic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Otosclerosis is most common in young-to-middle-aged females and is accelerated by pregnancy; 50 percent of cases are autosomal dominant with variable penetrance. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Why are nasal polyps classed as non-neoplastic?","choices":["They are malignant but slow growing","No new tissue is grown; existing mucosa becomes edematous","They arise from cartilage rather than epithelium","They regress without treatment in every case"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nasal polyps are benign edematous masses of the mucous membranes of the sinus ostia or ethmoid air cells, not new neoplastic tissue. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which nodal findings require immediate assessment to exclude lymphoma?","choices":["Tender mobile nodes during a viral upper respiratory infection","Bilateral small nodes in a child with otitis media","Persistent rubbery or matted nodes in older adults","Any palpable cervical node at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Reactive nodes are usually tender and follow a regional infection. Rubbery, matted, persistent and supraclavicular in an older patient is the combination that changes the level of concern. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which definitions of acute and chronic rhinosinusitis are accurate?","choices":["Acute under 2 weeks; chronic beyond 6 weeks, often with septal deviation","Acute under 4 weeks; chronic beyond 12 weeks despite therapy, often with nasal polyps","Acute under 12 weeks; chronic beyond 6 months, often with turbinate hypertrophy","Acute under 4 weeks; chronic beyond 12 weeks, usually with no mucosal change"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The two thresholds are the definition. Acute disease is usually viral &mdash; rhinovirus or influenza &mdash; with secondary bacterial infection by Streptococcus pneumoniae or Haemophilus influenzae. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which description fits the semicircular canals?","choices":["Linear acceleration and gravitational position, via the otoliths","Rotational acceleration, by endolymph bending the cupula","Sound frequency, via the basilar membrane","Middle ear pressure, via the Eustachian tube"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Three loops in the sagittal, coronal and transverse planes. The fluid lags behind the head, the gelatinous cupula bends, and the hair cell stereocilia inside it are stimulated. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why does presbycusis make voices hard to discriminate in noisy environments?","choices":["Low frequencies go first, taking the vowels","All frequencies fall equally","Only frequencies above the speech range are affected","High frequencies go first, taking the speech consonants"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Presbycusis begins with high-frequency tones, which include speech consonants such as /s/, /f/ and /t/, making voice discrimination difficult in noisy environments. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which single event in otosclerosis produces the hearing loss?","choices":["Ankylosis of the stapes footplate","Erosion of the incus by the remodeled bone","Hair cell loss at the cochlear base","Accumulation of endolymph in the scala media"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Once the footplate is fixed it cannot transfer mechanical vibration into the cochlear fluid, and conduction fails progressively. Fixation is the whole lesion. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What drives dizziness or lightheadedness, as distinct from vertigo?","choices":["Asymmetrical input between the two vestibular apparatus","Dislodged otoconia in the semicircular canals","Endolymphatic hydrops","Cerebral hypoperfusion or metabolic imbalance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dizziness is a non-vestibular sensation of impending faint or unsteadiness, driven by cerebral hypoperfusion, orthostatic hypotension or metabolic imbalance. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which description defines vertigo as distinct from dizziness?","choices":["A sensation of impending faint from cerebral hypoperfusion","Any unsteadiness on standing","Loss of consciousness preceded by spinning","A hallucination of motion, with NO syncope"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Asymmetry between the two sides is the mechanism, and the absence of syncope is the separator. Dizziness or lightheadedness is a non-vestibular sensation of impending faint. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What makes the middle ear vulnerable to the pressure changes behind otitis media?","choices":["It is fluid-filled and cannot compress","It is an air-filled space vented only by the Eustachian tube","It has no blood supply","It is lined by keratinized skin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A sealed air pocket with one vent is why closing the vent drops the pressure and draws in fluid. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which molecular pathway drives nasal polyp formation?","choices":["Interleukins 1, 6 and tumor necrosis factor, with neutrophil influx","Interferon gamma, with lymphocyte influx","Interleukins 4, 5 and 13, with eosinophil influx","Interleukin 17, with mast cell influx"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic type 2 allergic responses release interleukins 4, 5 and 13 and draw eosinophils into the tissue, forming benign edematous masses from the sinus ostia or ethmoid air cells. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What happens to the wider nasal cavity opposite a septal deviation?","choices":["Compensatory inferior turbinate hypertrophy","It atrophies from disuse","It develops polyps from the increased flow","Nothing changes; only the narrow side is affected"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The contralateral wide cavity undergoes compensatory inferior turbinate enlargement to humidify the increased volume of air passing through it. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which statement about bacterial tonsillitis and its abscess complication is accurate?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae; bilateral exudate","Pseudomonas aeruginosa; fever above 39 degrees","Haemophilus influenzae; odynophagia","Group A Streptococcus; asymmetric deviation with trismus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tonsillitis is usually Group A Streptococcus, and asymmetric tonsillar deviation with trismus indicates a complicating tonsillar abscess. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which ion movement depolarizes the cochlear hair cell?","choices":["Efflux into the perilymph through voltage-gated channels","Influx from the endolymph through the tip-link channels","Influx from the perilymph across the basilar membrane","Release from intracellular stores after glutamate binding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Deflected stereocilia open the tip-link channels, permitting rapid influx from the endolymph into the hair cell. Depolarization then opens voltage-gated channels and glutamate is released onto cranial nerve VIII. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which structure separates the external ear from the middle ear?","choices":["The oval window, which passes energy to the cochlea","The round window, which relieves pressure","The Eustachian tube, which equalizes pressure","The tympanic membrane, which vibrates and passes energy to the ossicles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is the boundary and the first moving part of the conducting chain, which is why perforating it causes conductive loss. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why does the vertigo of Meniere disease come in attacks rather than continuously?","choices":["Pressure builds until micro-ruptures occur, then re-equilibrates","The virus replicates in waves","Otoconia settle between episodes","Inflammation resolves and recurs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Defective resorption lets endolymph accumulate and balloon the scala media until micro-ruptures occur; that build-up and release makes it episodic. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How do cisplatin and carboplatin cause permanent bilateral sensorineural loss?","choices":["They generate reactive oxygen species that destroy outer hair cells at the cochlear base","They inhibit prestin in outer hair cells","They cross-link DNA in stria vascularis cells, compromising endolymph ion homeostasis","They fix the stapes footplate in the oval window"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The stria vascularis maintains the ionic composition of endolymph, and without that gradient transduction fails. Damage there is permanent and bilateral. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which statement about the causes of otitis externa is accurate?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae; fungal disease follows swimming","Moraxella catarrhalis alone; fungal disease follows trauma","Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus; fungal after antibiotics","Respiratory syncytial virus and rhinovirus; fungal disease follows steroid use"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus cause 80 to 90 percent of cases; fungal disease from Aspergillus niger or Candida albicans often follows prolonged antibiotic use. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which set of causes belongs to sensorineural rather than conductive loss?","choices":["Presbycusis, ototoxic drugs, noise trauma and acoustic neuroma","Cerumen impaction, otosclerosis, otitis media and tympanic membrane perforation","Cholesteatoma, exostoses, foreign body and ossicular necrosis","Eustachian tube dysfunction, middle ear effusion and stapes fixation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All four damage the cochlea or the nerve. Note acoustic neuroma sits here because cranial nerve VIII is part of the sensorineural pathway even though the tumor is outside the cochlea. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which two ototoxic effects are reversible or symptom-limited rather than permanent?","choices":["Gentamicin and tobramycin damage","Cisplatin and carboplatin damage","Noise trauma and presbycusis","Furosemide on the strial potential; salicylate on prestin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The loop diuretic effect on the strial potential is reversible, and the salicylate effect on the outer hair cell motor protein produces tinnitus. Neither destroys the cells outright. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Why does prolonged antibiotic use predispose to FUNGAL otitis externa?","choices":["It thins the canal skin directly","It removes the bacterial competition that normally limits fungal growth","It raises the canal pH to favor fungi","It damages the tympanic membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prolonged antibiotic use suppresses the bacteria, and Aspergillus niger or Candida albicans then take over as fungal otitis externa. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which way does the nystagmus of labyrinthitis beat?","choices":["Horizontal-rotary, with the fast phase beating TOWARD the affected side","Vertical and non-suppressible","Absent, because hearing rather than balance is affected","Horizontal-rotary, with the fast phase beating AWAY from the affected side"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Labyrinthitis gives horizontal-rotary nystagmus with the fast phase beating away from the affected side, with severe postural instability, gait ataxia and nausea. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of Meniere disease?","choices":["Defective endolymph resorption causing hydrops, ballooning the scala media until micro-ruptures occur","Inflammatory swelling and vascular congestion of the labyrinth after a viral infection","Dislodged otoconia floating in the semicircular canals","Demyelination of the vestibular nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Endolymphatic hydrops is the lesion: fluid accumulates because it is not resorbed, the membranous labyrinth distends, and micro-ruptures follow. The episodic nature follows from that cycle. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why does anticoagulation favor POSTERIOR rather than anterior epistaxis?","choices":["The anterior septum has no blood supply","Anticoagulants are concentrated posteriorly","Posterior bleeding is driven by systemic vascular and clotting factors","The posterior mucosa is thinner"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anterior causes are local insults to the mucosa; posterior causes act on the vessels and on clotting. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 23"}]};
